Examine the damper for notches or markings. Some dampers have a set of notches, or arrows, that indicate "Slow/fast". If your dampers have markers like this, use them to determine how far back you want to rotate your dampers.
Some dampers have no notches or markings; if this is the case, you will have to guess at how far back to rotate them. You may need to repeat this step a few times to perfect the tightness of your dampers.
If there is only one set of notches on the damper, it can not be adjusted.
Once you have determined how far back to rotate your damper, insert the large end of the damper back into the hinge assembly, leaving the small end sticking out. This will make it easier to rotate the small end of the damper.', '{"step_id": 281199, "order": 5, "title": "", "lines": [{"text_raw": "Hold one of the dampers by the smaller end.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Hold one of the dampers by the smaller end."}, {"text_raw": "Examine the damper for notches or markings. Some dampers have a set of notches, or arrows, that indicate \"Slow/fast\". If your dampers have markers like this, use them to determine how far back you want to rotate your dampers.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Examine the damper for notches or markings. Some dampers have a set of notches, or arrows, that indicate &quot;Slow/fast&quot;. If your dampers have markers like this, use them to determine how far back you want to rotate your dampers."}, {"text_raw": "Some dampers have no notches or markings; if this is the case, you will have to guess at how far back to rotate them. You may need to repeat this step a few times to perfect the tightness of your dampers.", "bullet": "icon_note", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Some dampers have no notches or markings; if this is the case, you will have to guess at how far back to rotate them. You may need to repeat this step a few times to perfect the tightness of your dampers."}, {"text_raw": "If there is only one set of notches on the damper, it can not be adjusted.", "bullet": "icon_note", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "If there is only one set of notches on the damper, it can not be adjusted."}, {"text_raw": "Once you have determined how far back to rotate your damper, insert the large end of the damper back into the hinge assembly, leaving the small end sticking out. This will make it easier to rotate the small end of the damper.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Once you have determined how far back to rotate your damper, insert the large end of the damper back into the hinge assembly, leaving the small end sticking out. This will make it easier to rotate the small end of the damper."}]}') ON CONFLICT (document_id, step_number) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, body=EXCLUDED.body, metadata=EXCLUDED.metadata;

Pinch the protruding end of the damper with the needle-nose pliers and twist, rotating it in the direction of the plastic hinge covers. Rotate the dampers until they reach the level of tension you determined in Step 5.
Repeat steps 5-6 with the other damper.', '{"step_id": 281205, "order": 6, "title": "", "lines": [{"text_raw": "Grip your needle-nose pliers in one hand and the hinge assembly in the other.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Grip your needle-nose pliers in one hand and the hinge assembly in the other."}, {"text_raw": "Pinch the protruding end of the damper with the needle-nose pliers and twist, rotating it in the direction of the plastic hinge covers. Rotate the dampers until they reach the level of tension you determined in Step 5.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Pinch the protruding end of the damper with the needle-nose pliers and twist, rotating it in the direction of the plastic hinge covers. Rotate the dampers until they reach the level of tension you determined in Step 5."}, {"text_raw": "Repeat steps 5-6 with the other damper.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Repeat steps 5-6 with the other damper."}]}') ON CONFLICT (document_id, step_number) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, body=EXCLUDED.body, metadata=EXCLUDED.metadata;

Re-insert the damper, small-end first, with your fingers. You should be able to feel the damper sliding into the hinge hole in the toilet seat.
Do the same to the other damper.
If you removed a plastic cover from the hinge assembly, snap it back into place.
With the hinge assembly and toilet seat re-assembled, pick the entire toilet seat back up and align it with the bolts on your toilet bowl.
Push straight forward, until the hinges audibly click back into place.
Finally, close the plastic hinge covers.', '{"step_id": 281206, "order": 7, "title": "", "lines": [{"text_raw": "Place the hinge assembly back into the toilet seat with the holes aligned.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Place the hinge assembly back into the toilet seat with the holes aligned."}, {"text_raw": "Re-insert the damper, small-end first, with your fingers. You should be able to feel the damper sliding into the hinge hole in the toilet seat.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Re-insert the damper, small-end first, with your fingers. You should be able to feel the damper sliding into the hinge hole in the toilet seat."}, {"text_raw": "Do the same to the other damper.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Do the same to the other damper."}, {"text_raw": "If you removed a plastic cover from the hinge assembly, snap it back into place.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "If you removed a plastic cover from the hinge assembly, snap it back into place."}, {"text_raw": "With the hinge assembly and toilet seat re-assembled, pick the entire toilet seat back up and align it with the bolts on your toilet bowl.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "With the hinge assembly and toilet seat re-assembled, pick the entire toilet seat back up and align it with the bolts on your toilet bowl."}, {"text_raw": "Push straight forward, until the hinges audibly click back into place.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Push straight forward, until the hinges audibly click back into place."}, {"text_raw": "Finally, close the plastic hinge covers.", "bullet": "black", "level": 0, "lineid": null, "text_rendered": "Finally, close the plastic hinge covers."}]}') ON CONFLICT (document_id, step_number) DO UPDATE SET title=EXCLUDED.title, body=EXCLUDED.body, metadata=EXCLUDED.metadata;


Safety precautions

UNPLUG POWER CORD; TURN OFF WATER SUPPLY.

Check machine details on the inside of the main door.

Remove top cover

Move washing machine away from wall to make rear and front accessible.

Using Torx T20, unscrew two screws (top left and top right on rear side) .

Slide top backwards, lift it off, and put it aside.

Remove detergent dispensing tray, and 3 screws

Push down blue part in the middle to release the tray, and pull it out completely.

Unscrew three T20 screws; the one on the lower right is shorter.

Remove control panel

Unhook three hooks at the top (two shown).

On the inside, on the right, there is another hook (marked), sitting between two guiding notches without hooks.

Disconnect control panel

Release the cables by gently pressing the hooked guides. Note that the black part (marked by a rectangle) must be unlocked.

Finally, unlock the connector (e.g. with your nails or a flat-blade screwdriver) and pull it out.

Put the control panel aside.

Immobilize the tumbler unit

The drum is freely suspended. Wedge a towel on the rear side behind the drum to immobilize it. This will prevent damage when tilting the washing machine later on.

DO NOT FORGET TO REMOVE THIS when reassembling!

Detach door seal from front cover

The rubber door seal is attached to the front cover by a metal wire and a spring.

Pry out the spring and pull away the wire.

Unpeel the seal all the way round, and tuck it inside.

Remove drain/filter door, drain water, and unscrew

The round door (front bottom right) for the drain tube and filter can be removed, by opening it completely, and gently pulling out the left side of the hinge.

DRAIN ANY REMAINING WATER .

Unscrew two T20 screws.

Unscrew front cover

Unscrew two (T20) screws at the top.

Tilt the whole washing machine towards its rear side to expose the front bottom. Lean it against a wall.

Unscrew two (6mm hex-head) screws on the bottom.

Remove front cover

The front cover is held in place by plastic hooked pins. Slightly lift the front cover to unhook it.

On the inside, unclip something, and pull out a connector (for the door lock). The plastic emergency door release comes out easily. The image shows this when clip and connector are already disconnected.

DO NOT FORGET TO PUT ALL THIS BACK on reassembly!

Remove plastic strip

Remove the plastic strip that protects the bottom edge.

Unhook on the back, and tilt it forwards.

Note the guiding notches to help you put it back.

Remove drain/filter cover plate

Unscrew one (T20) screw.

Tilt the plate back, and lift it. There is a hooked tab, and a guiding pin to help you reassemble the plate.

Pull out the drain tube.

Put the cover plate aside.

Disconnect pump

Pull the power connector from the pump.

Using pliers, loosen the clamp on the hose from the tumbler and slide it back.

Remove the hose.

BE PREPARED FOR SOME WATER (use a towel or bowl).

Remove debris

In my case, the pump inlet (where we just removed the hose coming from the tumbler) was clogged with debris and some cloth.

This prevented draining, and even prevented removal of the filter. Thus, requiring disassembly. But we are not done yet.

Detach filter/pump unit

Unscrew four T20 screws.

Tilt the filter/pump unit (note the tab at the top), and pull it out from the back.

Clean inlet, filter, and pump

At his point you can clean the water inlet, the filter, and the pump. The impeller should move freely. The pump itself can also be removed to replace it (but I did not do that).

In my case, a significant amount of cloth got stuck in the water inlet, including a necklace. In the filter, there were some hairpins, and inside the pump itself, a coin and a pendent. LESSON: CLEAN THE FILTER MORE OFTEN, while you can stil remove it without disassembling the machine.', 679, 'https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/Washing+Machine+Disassembly+to+Access+Water+Drain+Pump/105443', NULL, '{"license": "CC-BY-NC-SA-3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/", "rights_status": null, "rights_url": null, "usage_status": "project_noncommercial_status_unconfirmed"}', '{"id": "105443", "group": "ifixit", "title_zh": "拆卸洗衣机以检修排水泵", "title": "Washing Machine Disassembly to Access Water Drain Pump", "english_words": 679, "characters_with_whitespace": 3924, "characters_without_whitespace": 3182, "steps": 16, "source_url": "https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/Washing+Machine+Disassembly+to+Access+Water+Drain+Pump/105443", "text_path": "texts/105443.txt", "source_metadata": {"category": "washer_drain_access", "guide_id": 105443, "api_url": "https://www.ifixit.com/api/2.0/guides/105443", "status": "success", "quality_accepted": false, "knowledge_base_imported": false, "usage_approved": false, "license": "CC-BY-NC-SA-3.0", "license_url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/3.0/", "usage_status": "project_noncommercial_status_unconfirmed", "record_type": "repair_guide_not_work_order", "source_url": "https://www.ifixit.com/Guide/Washing+Machine+Disassembly+to+Access+Water+Drain+Pump/105443", "title": "Washing Machine Disassembly to Access Water Drain Pump", "author": {"userid": 2449350, "username": "Tom Verhoeff", "unique_username": "wstomv", "join_date": 1522521620, "image": {"id": -32, "guid": "default_avatar_placeholder_guid", "original": "https://assets.cdn.ifixit.com/static/images/avatars/User/ifixit/avatar-9", "mini": "https://assets.cdn.ifixit.com/static/images/avatars/User/ifixit/avatar-9.mini", "thumbnail": "https://assets.cdn.ifixit.com/s